From 34cd8e8a96cf5536f190dd8030f6a4b90cc397ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:24:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] Keep decoded frames alive when their context is destroyed

On a mid-stream resolution change FFmpeg destroys the decode context while
frames from it still sit in its filter queue. DestroyContext munmapped and
closed every CAPTURE buffer, so the next vaGetImage of such a frame read
freed memory: SIGSEGV on all three resolution-change vectors (H.264, HEVC,
VP9).

Now DestroyContext first submits any held-back frame and waits for the
decodes in flight, because STREAMOFF would discard them. Then it hands
each decoded frame's CAPTURE buffer to its surface as standalone backing.
The dma-buf fds and the mapping move over instead of being closed, and
they keep the vb2 memory alive after the queue is freed. A driver-wide
rwlock keeps readers (images, exports) out while that happens.

decode-reinit-test: 0/3 -> 3/3, every frame bit-exact against software.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/43b2cdd4aa1b161a281db8b10f02ce70085e2d42/patches/libva-v4l2_request/0003-Keep-decoded-frames-alive-when-their-context-is-dest.patch (0003-Keep-decoded-frames-alive-when-their-context-is-dest.patch, commit ae94ce3)
